Z||000012||Indiana_resto_guy||04-28-2001||12:12 AM||||The module theogre refers to is indeed in the distributor. (I agree with this, as do others I'm sure)<BR>It is the flat black one that has connectors from the coil and the main wiring harness, and plugs into the pick-up coil inside.<BR>If you take it and get it tested it WILL check good cold. When they build up heat during operation they quit!<BR>Go and get a new one,(OEM equipment!) not after market.||||reg||1
Z||000013||jhar1987||04-28-2001||12:20 AM||||thanx indy, thats my next step! "fingers crossed"..jeff||||reg||1
Z||000014||WKDFIRO||04-28-2001||12:55 AM||||My money is still on the coil for a quick fix and the pick up coil for the long term fix. At least on my car if I neglect the P/U coil, I start going through ignition coils.<P>Good luck!||||reg||7
Z||000015||Ed||04-28-2001||09:02 PM||||My Caravan's in-tank fuel pump was very loud for several months before it failed.  I even asked two Dodge service managers about the noise on two separate occasions, and whether the pump was ok.  They said it was, and it wasn't.<P>On a related subject, ignition modules.<P>Why were the ford drivers succesful in getting a recall on their modules?!  Many people here, including me, have had their modules fail suddenly. We even talk about carrying spares (I do).  <P>Let's all file for a recall and get our modules moved to a cooler place!<p>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 04-28-2001).]||||reg||1
